      ":question/id": "785c649d-2af7-5e75-8cea-f384d669f8b1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}=- 2 y \\sin{(x )} + 4\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}=- 2 y \\sin{(x )} + 4 \\\\ 3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 2 y \\sin{(x )}=4 \\cos{(x )} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\frac{2 \\sin{(x )}}{3})}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{\\frac{4 \\cos{(x )}}{3} + \\frac{4}{3}}_{Q(x)}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{2 \\sin{(x )}}{3}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{4 \\cos{(x )}}{3} + \\frac{4}{3}.$",
